    not_duplicate_of: []  # 938f
    pr_ref:  # 938f
    description: "FOUND by shipping INV-pusin's fix on a branch and MEASURING THE PUBLISHED\nOUTPUT before merging it. The fix is correct in itself and the item is now\nBLOCKED ON THIS ONE, because closing INV-pusin without closing this converts a\nright-for-the-wrong-reason `inconclusive` into a FALSE `confirmed`.\n\nTHE FIXTURE, authored and already on disk at\n~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/rust_fidelity_probe_08232026/repo (zero dependencies,\nno build.rs):\n\n    pub fn exfiltrate(sock: &UdpSocket, payload: &[u8]) -> io::Result<usize> {\n        sock.send(payload)          // std::net::UdpSocket.send  -> net_send\n    }\n    pub fn drain<W: Write>(w: &mut W, payload: &[u8]) -> io::Result<()> {\n        w.write_all(payload)        // std::io::Write.write_all  -> fs_write\n    }\n    pub fn load_config(path: &str) -> io::Result<String> {\n        fs::read_to_string(path)    // CONTROL, fires violated in every run\n    }\n\nBEFORE INV-pusin's fix:  net_send and fs_write are `inconclusive`, withheld by\nthe uncatalogued-module gate over producer artifacts (`std`, `std.io`,\n`std.net`) that have nothing to do with either call.\nAFTER INV-pusin's fix:   both are bare `confirmed` -- \"No net_send chains\nfound.\" / \"No fs_write chains found.\" -- while the control still fires\n`violated`, so the analysis is demonstrably not blind. It looked, saw neither\ncall, and reported the silence as safety about a function named `exfiltrate`.\n\nTWO INDEPENDENT MECHANISMS, both measured, both required:\n\n(1) A METHOD-NAME DENYLIST SUPPRESSES THE EDGE ENTIRELY.\n    rust.py's _RUST_GENERIC_TRAIT_METHODS (77 names) blocks the instance-method\n    shape `receiver.method()` for ubiquitous names. Ten of rust.yaml's 34\n    method-kind I/O sinks are on it:\n\n      fs_write    std::io::Write.write, std::io::Write.flush\n      net_send    std::net::UdpSocket.send, std::net::TcpStream.write\n      net_recv    std::net::UdpSocket.recv, std::net::TcpStream.read\n      subprocess  std::process::Command.new/.output/.spawn/.status\n\n    Probed per name on an annotated receiver: `s.send(b)` emits NO EDGE in\n    three syntactic positions (tail expression, `.unwrap()` chain, `let _ =`),\n    while `s.send_to(...)` and even `s.zzz_unknown(...)` both emit. So the\n    denylist, not resolution difficulty, is what drops it.\n\n    THE SUBPROCESS ROW ABOVE IS NOT A LIVE GAP AND THE FIRST DRAFT OF THIS ITEM\n    SAID IT WAS. A live control refutes it: a crate calling\n    `Command::new(\"sh\").arg(..).status()` returns `violated`, 1 subprocess\n    chain, and the fs_read claim is correctly withheld naming\n    `std::process::Command.new` as an opaque launch. The denylist governs the\n    INSTANCE-METHOD path only; `Command::new` is an associated-function call\n    (`Type::method`) on a different path, and it catches the chain first. The\n    genuinely unreachable set is the SIX rows whose only real call shape is\n    `receiver.method()`.\n\n    This is a VOCABULARY CONFLICT, not a stray literal: the denylist exists for\n    precision (WI-bakak, 22 false positives from `.load()`) and the catalogue\n    exists for recall, and they disagree about ten names with no arbiter. The\n    fix is NOT to delete the denylist -- measurement 0001's band sequences\n    recall work behind precision. Resolving it properly needs the receiver TYPE,\n    which is a fidelity question (see the fidelity-provenance item): with\n    rust-analyzer the receiver is known and the catalogue can win safely;\n    without it, it cannot.\n\n(2) THE DISCLOSURE THAT SHOULD CATCH WHAT (1) DROPS IS INERT FOR RUST.\n    `w.write_all(x)` is NOT denylisted and DOES emit --\n    `rust:external:0-0:write_all:unresolved` -- but the module slot is the bare\n    `external` placeholder, so it classifies against nothing. INV-fibis's\n    untyped-receiver caveat exists for exactly this and cannot fire, because\n    `untyped_receiver_sites` requires `meta.call_construct == \"method\"` and\n    EVERY rust call edge measured carries `call_construct=None`. Rust therefore\n    reaches a clean verdict with no caveat channel of any kind.\n\nRELATION TO WI-nasuf. Same family, different language, and RUST IS NOT IN THAT\nITEM'S TABLE -- it measured java / scala / swift / python / kotlin / javascript\nonly. Rust's version is also not identical: kotlin and javascript emit NO edge\nfor ANY external instance-method call, while rust emits for most and drops a\ndenylisted ten. Filed here rather than appended there so the denylist mechanism\nis not lost inside a \"no edge emitted\" summary.\n\nWHAT THIS COSTS TODAY, stated so the priority is arguable rather than asserted:\nnothing, because the accidental gate is still standing. It costs everything the\nmoment INV-pusin lands. That is the whole reason this is filed instead of\nmerged around.\n\nNOT MEASURED: how many of the ten denylisted names actually appear as sinks in\nreal Rust corpora; whether the rust-analyzer/SCIP path resolves the receiver\nwell enough to lift the denylist safely; whether cpp has the same\ndenylist-versus-catalogue conflict; the false-positive cost of stamping\n`call_construct` on rust method calls."  # 938f
    fields:  # 938f
      statement: "A clean boundary verdict on a Rust repo must either see the catalogued sink method or disclose that it could not: no analyzer-internal method-name denylist may silently remove a call site that the language's I/O catalogue declares as a sink, and every external method call the analyzer cannot resolve must carry the call_construct stamp the untyped-receiver disclosure reads."  # 938f
      root_cause: "Two independent mechanisms, both required for the false confirm. (1) rust.py _RUST_GENERIC_TRAIT_METHODS is a 77-name denylist on the instance-method call path, added for precision (WI-bakak), that overlaps rust.yaml's declared sink methods on ten names including UdpSocket.send and io::Write.write -- two vocabularies naming the same methods with no arbiter, and the denylist silently wins. (2) The rust analyzer never stamps meta.call_construct, and verify_claims.untyped_receiver_sites requires call_construct == 'method', so INV-fibis's untyped-receiver caveat can never fire for rust; an edge that does survive the denylist (write_all) lands on the bare 'external' placeholder and classifies against nothing, with no caveat channel to disclose it."  # 938f
- op: discuss  # c6f3
  at: '2026-08-24T03:51:30Z'  # c6f3
  by: agent  # c6f3
  actor: jgstern_agent  # c6f3
  clock: 2  # c6f3
  nonce: c6f3  # c6f3
  message: "OWNER RULING 2026-08-23, in their words: \"declare the blindness, do those 4 PRs.\"\n\nWHAT THIS SETTLES. Kotlin and JavaScript get a DECLARED BLINDNESS RESIDUAL NOW.\nThey are not held until WI-nasuf builds the missing edges. A dated per-language\ndeclaration (\"this analyzer does not emit external instance-method call edges\")\nis consumed by the coverage gate, which then refuses a bare `confirmed` for\nthose languages. The owner accepted the visible cost: a verdict that says in as\nmany words that hypergumbo cannot see a language's method calls at all.\n\nWHY IT IS THE RIGHT SHAPE, recorded so it is not re-litigated. It is the same\nPOSITIVE DATED DECLARATION form already ruled for stdlib completeness, and it\nmeans WI-nasuf later flips a DECLARATION rather than the invariant's colour --\nwhich is exactly the anti-correlation (LIVE.md rule 19) the bar rewrite was\nordered to kill. Under a capability bar, building the kotlin edges would have\nread as a mass new violation.\n\nTHE MEASUREMENT THAT PROMPTED IT, saved at\n~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/disclosure_parity_08232026/ (fixtures + probe +\nresults). One fixture per language, each calling a sink from that language's\nOWN catalogue on a receiver the analyzer cannot type:\n\n  python, go                          disclosure FIRES              168 sinks\n  java, scala, swift, objc, rust      edge emitted, NO stamp        554 sinks\n  kotlin, javascript                  NO EDGE AT ALL                232 sinks\n\n  786 of 954 method-kind sinks (82%) are in languages where INV-fibis's\n  untyped-receiver disclosure structurally cannot fire. Method-kind is nearly\n  the whole catalogue for several: java 98%, kotlin 97%, objc 95%, swift 93%,\n  scala 89%.\n\nTHE FOUR PRs, in order:\n  PR1  stamp call_construct on the unresolved-external method path in java,\n       scala, swift, objc, rust (the edge already exists; only the meta key is\n       missing) + a REGISTRY-DRIVEN PARITY GATE: every language with\n       method-kind sinks needs a fixture whose untypable receiver produces a\n       caveat, and a new language with no fixture FAILS the gate. That gate is\n       the anti-corner-cutting device -- without it language number ten goes\n       silently blind and nothing turns red.\n  PR2  the declared blindness above (kotlin, javascript).\n  PR3  disclose the denylist rather than delete it -- a call site dropped\n       because its name is on _RUST_GENERIC_TRAIT_METHODS *and* matches a\n       catalogued sink name is exactly \"something I declined to examine\".\n       Precision preserved (measurement 0001's band stands). Unblocks the\n       held branch jgstern-agent/fix/scoped-attr-ref-import-and-type-positions\n       @9221f81664 and therefore INV-pusin.\n  PR4  WI-lagod, fidelity provenance -- the literal second half of the bar.\n\nTHEN CLOSE INV-fibis / INV-nuhun with behavioral evidence: each item's own\nfiled repro re-run on the merged tree, the falsifiability control (a fixture\nwith nothing to disclose must still return bare `confirmed`, zero caveats),\nand the nine-language parity table regenerated AS A TEST ARTIFACT rather than\na one-off measurement.\n\nREJECTED, and recorded so it stays rejected: narrowing the statements to\n\"languages that emit typed receivers\". It would close both items immediately\nand it is the same scope-narrowing that produced this residual in the first\nplace -- INV-fibis's own root_cause records the residual was originally filed\nas a KOTLIN-specific problem and is not language-specific."  # c6f3
- op: discuss  # d831
  at: '2026-08-24T04:01:20Z'  # d831
  by: agent  # d831
  actor: jgstern_agent  # d831
  clock: 3  # d831
  nonce: d831  # d831
  message: "CORRECTION TO MY OWN MEASUREMENT, before it hardens into a fact anyone builds\non. The 82% figure in the ruling entry above is WRONG. The real figure is 55%,\nand five-languages-need-stamps is really THREE.\n\nWHAT I GOT WRONG. My first fixtures for scala and swift used a CAST receiver\n(f.asInstanceOf[java.io.File].createNewFile(), (fm as! FileManager)\n.createFile(...)). A cast expression takes a different, BARE-CALL branch in\nboth analyzers. The plain `receiver.method()` shape -- the one real code uses\n-- is ALREADY STAMPED CORRECTLY in scala and swift. Re-measured on the plain\nshape:\n\n    python  FIRES     java    INERT (edge emitted, no stamp)\n    go      FIRES     objc    INERT\n    scala   FIRES     rust    INERT\n    swift   FIRES     kotlin  NO EDGE      javascript  NO EDGE\n\n    954 method-kind sinks total\n    295 inert but fixable by a stamp : java 139, objc 122, rust 34\n    232 no edge at all (WI-nasuf)    : kotlin 181, javascript 51\n    527 blind in total = 55%, not 82%\n    427 already firing               : scala 162, python 143, swift 97, go 25\n\nThis is LIVE.md rule 12 in its exact filed form -- a one-arm test of the WRONG\nARM generalises to a falsehood -- and it was caught by instrumenting which\nemit site actually fired rather than by reading the analyzers.\n\nNOTHING ABOUT THE RULING CHANGES. The gap is smaller and the work is smaller;\nthe shape is identical, and 55% of catalogued method sinks reaching a silent\nclean verdict is not a defensible position either.\n\nPR1 IS DONE AND MEASURED AT THREE LAYERS.\n  unit     : the registry-driven parity gate, 9 tests green, and it is driven\n             from the shipped io_primitives catalogues so a language arriving\n             later with method-kind sinks and no fixture FAILS instead of\n             going silently blind.\n  analyzer : java / objc / rust now stamp call_construct on the\n             unresolved-external method path. java's stamp moved OUT of the\n             typed-receiver branch (where it meant \"a method call whose\n             receiver I typed\") to the one place every receiver-bearing call\n             passes; objc stamps unconditionally because a message send is a\n             method call by construction; rust stamps on is_method_call.\n  published: java went from a BARE `confirmed` (rc 0) to\n             `confirmed_with_caveats` (rc 3) -- \"At 2 of 2 method call site(s)\n             the receiver's type could not be determined ... distinct\n             method(s): u.mkdirs, xs.size\". The CONTROL ARM (same claim, a\n             TYPED `java.io.File` receiver) stays `violated` rc 1, so this is\n             not a blanket downgrade. objc likewise moved to\n             confirmed_with_caveats rc 3.\n\nRECALL COST: none, and gate_named_entry is where you can see why -- with no\nmodule hint it refuses a method-kind hit EITHER WAY, through the\n`call_construct == \"method\"` arm with the stamp and through the `non_method`\nfilter without it. The stamp only buys the ability to SAY SO.\n\nTWO RESIDUALS FOUND AT LAYER 3, filed on the PR1 item rather than fixed here:\njava's callee name carries the receiver prefix (`u.mkdirs`), so only the\nUNSCOPED `unknown_receiver_scope` caveat fires and the boundary-scoped\n`untyped_receiver` one cannot match the catalogue's `mkdirs`; and objc's\ncaveat renders its method name as empty (\"distinct method(s): .\") even though\nparts[3] of the id is `createFileAtPath`, so the cause is elsewhere and is NOT\nyet located. rust's layer-3 arm still returns `inconclusive` for an unrelated\nreason -- WI-lutuh, rust.yaml declaring zero module_completeness -- so its\nstamp is verified at the unit layer only."  # d831
